=== REASONING ===

Why this design preserves ADR-015, the Phase-50 P1..P10 invariants,
and the non-AI baseline:

  1. Single detector (P10 baseline coexistence).
     The deterministic Z-score + range + trend detector is the
     canonical baseline. The slice REFACTORS GetAnomalies to delegate
     to a new public method DetectAnomalies that returns the typed
     aitools.AnomalyContextResult; the HTTP handler then converts
     back to the legacy private wire shape via
     anomalyContextResultToResponse. There is exactly ONE detector,
     period. No threshold value was changed; no SQL was added or
     duplicated. The graceful-degradation contract (per-stage query
     failures are logged + swallowed; the method always returns
     non-nil result with nil error) is preserved verbatim.

     Selection mechanism for AI vs baseline:
        Off mode (ai_mode='off'): the AI route is 404 (guard.Wrap
                                  short-circuits BEFORE the handler)
                                  and the AI UI section is absent
                                  (withAiFeature returns null). The
                                  baseline detector + static
                                  safe-range explanation strings keep
                                  rendering as before.
        On mode (ai_mode in ['local','cloud']) AND
        anomaly-explanations toggle = true:
                                  the baseline detector + static
                                  messages STILL render. The opt-in
                                  AI section renders ALONGSIDE them
                                  with a Generate button. The AI does
                                  not replace any baseline content —
                                  it ADDS a narration card.

  2. Read-only tool (F4).
     internal/ai/tools/anomaly.go ships ONE tool,
     `query_anomaly_context`, with Mutates() == false and
     RequiredScope() == "". The strategy's allowedTools whitelist
     contains only this tool name. The dispatcher's deny-all confirm
     hook (denyAllConfirm shared with chatbot/digest/yir handlers)
     closes the door if a future strategy edit accidentally adds a
     mutating tool.

  3. Decorator chain (F5/F8/F9, locked order).
     The AI handler resolves the per-feature provider from the
     provider.Registry built at boot. The Registry.For chain wraps
     concrete adapters in the locked decorator order from the
     methodology:
        redact (F8) → rate-limit (F9) → cost-cap (F9) →
        audit (F3) → trace (observability)
     This slice does NOT bypass the chain — it goes through
     registry.For exactly like every prior U-tier slice. The
     redaction policy is PolicyDigest (per slice prompt's mandate
     to reuse it).

  4. F4 tool path for any context fetch — no parallel SQL.
     query_anomaly_context's Execute calls AnomalySource.DetectAnomalies
     and shapes the result into a flat envelope. The compile-time
     assertion in anomaly_handler.go pins the conformance:
        var _ aitools.AnomalySource = (*AnomalyHandler)(nil)
     A renamed method or signature change breaks the build at boot,
     surfacing the wiring bug at compile time.

  5. Registry metadata complete.
     internal/ai/features/registry.go gains an "anomaly-explanations"
     entry with every RouteSet field populated (Backend, Frontend,
     UITestIDs, JobNames=[], PushKinds=[]). Empty arrays are EXPLICIT
     (not nil) so features.CoverageOK() passes and tools/aivet's
     coverage proof has authoritative metadata to walk in off mode.

  6. Goldens (F6).
     internal/ai/strategies/anomaly-explanations/goldens.yaml ships
     three deterministic goldens — all_clear, one_critical_anomaly,
     refusal_other_vehicle — each backed by a canned mock-provider
     reply YAML. The harness (`go run ./cmd/ai-eval --feature
     anomaly-explanations`) runs them through the F6 evaluator,
     deterministically.

  7. Per-feature opt-in (I7).
     DefaultOn=false in the registry; the UI HOC (withAiFeature)
     reads ai_mode AND ai_features['anomaly-explanations']; the
     backend guard does the same. Both gates are independent —
     either being closed hides the surface.

  8. Hidden UI in off mode (I5).
     AIAnomalyExplanations is wrapped with withAiFeature so it
     returns null in off mode. The off-mode Vitest mounts the
     wrapped component with ai_mode='off' AND toggle=true and
     asserts the wrapper container is empty (no DOM nodes carrying
     the test ID). The positive-control case (mode='cloud',
     toggle=true) proves the gate actually fires.

  9. 404 routes in off mode (I6).
     internal/api/ai_routes.go mounts the route under guard.Wrap,
     and the off-mode Go test asserts the route returns 404 with
     an inner handler that would otherwise emit
     "GUARD_BYPASSED — handler should not have been called in off
     mode" + 500. A non-404 status is a guard-bypass bug.

 10. Type system (I10).
     The AI surface lives entirely under /api/v1/ai/*; the baseline
     /analytics/anomalies wire shape is byte-identical to the
     pre-refactor handler (verified by TestGetAnomalies_WireShapeUnchanged
     in internal/api/anomaly_handler_test.go which pins
     `"anomalies":[]` is emitted, not `null`, plus every field name
     and type).

 11. Client/bg surfaces (I12) NOT applicable.
     This slice ships zero new background jobs and zero new push
     kinds — anomaly explanation is request/response, narrated on
     demand. The empty arrays in the registry are explicit so
     CoverageOK does not flag them.

